// src/lex/lex.zen
// The lexer's surface: nothing but starred re-exports.
//
// A folder root is a file of starred bindings and nothing else — that is
// what makes a folder work, and it is why the eight files below can be
// reorganised without a single consumer changing a line.
//
// THE CONTRACT, in one paragraph. `scan(alloc, source)` reads bytes and
// hands back every token it could read together with every fault it found.
// It fails only when it cannot allocate. A file with faults still yields
// tokens, and the driver decides what to do with them — the rule this tree
// has held to is that a file with lexical diagnostics is REPORTED and NOT
// PARSED, because tree-sitter's opinion of the wreckage was only ever a
// second diagnostic for one mistake, and a hand-written parser's would be
// no better.
//
// THE TEN LEXICAL RULES, all of them from DESIGN.md's "Lexical rules", all
// of them the same shape — REJECT RATHER THAN REINTERPRET:
//
//   the escape set is exactly `\n \t \r \0 \\ \' \"`      lex_literal.escape
//   a string or char literal does not span lines,          lex_literal.string
//     and the diagnostic points at the OPENING QUOTE       lex_literal.character
//   a character literal holds exactly one byte             lex_literal.character
//   a digit may not be followed by an identifier byte      lex_literal.suffix
//   a leading zero is rejected; Zen has no octal           lex_literal.number
//   `12.` is an error; a float has digits on both sides    lex_literal.fraction
//   identifiers are ASCII                                  std.core.byte.is_ident_start
//   block comments do not nest                             lex_scan.block_comment
//   a BOM is stripped only at offset 0                     lex_scan.strip_bom
//   `@` is closed to `@Self`, `@meta`, `@scope`            lex_scan.at_kind
//
// Hex is not in v1, and that is a decision with a cost stated rather than
// hidden: bit masks are written in decimal until someone adds it. `0xFF` is
// not "a number followed by an identifier character" but its own token
// shape, and adding a token shape later is compatible in a way that
// removing one is not.

// what a token is, and where it came from
Pos*, Span*, TokenKind*, Token*, Source*  = lex.lex_token
text_of*, kind_name*                      = lex.lex_token

// what the lexer says when the bytes are wrong
LexFault*, Diag*, message*                = lex.lex_diag

// the alphabet, and the byte-order mark no string literal can spell
ByteClass*, class_of*, is_escape*         = lex.lex_byte
BOM_FIRST*, BOM_SECOND*, BOM_THIRD*       = lex.lex_byte

// the walk, and the maximal-munch table — exported because the parser's
// error recovery re-reads both, not because a caller needs them to lex
Cursor*, cursor_at*                       = lex.lex_cursor
punct_of*, punct_kind*                    = lex.lex_punct

// the scan in progress, and what it produced
Lexer*, Lexed*                            = lex.lex_state

// the digit accumulator, shared with the parser so a literal is read once
// by one implementation rather than twice by two
Digits*, digits_of*                       = lex.lex_literal

// THE ENTRY POINT
scan*                                     = lex.lex_scan
